WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.350
Vintage video games have become more popular
than ever as older generations of players

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:12.270
seek to revisit their old after-school activities,
and newer gamers want to see if Bayou Billy

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.010
was really that difficult.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:18.381
Most video games aren't worth a whole lot,
but there may be a few buried in your closet

00:00:18.381 --> 00:00:20.190
that are worth a few bucks.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:24.610
We'll skip the ultra-rare exclusive stuff
and focus on games you may have just picked

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:27.790
up at a store back in the day and have actually
played.

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:31.760
Since values always fluctuate, these prices
are based on information from the date of

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.000
this video, so future viewers, your experience
may vary.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.190
We’re also focusing on loose games, because
only nerds keep their video games sealed.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:45.020
Sealed and complete games, with their boxes
and manuals included, often command even higher

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.309
prices than the ones mentioned here.

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:50.769
Start digging through your collections as
we go through this treasure chest of valuable

00:00:50.769 --> 00:00:51.819
games!

00:00:51.819 --> 00:00:53.979
Stadium Events, U.S. Version

00:00:53.979 --> 00:01:00.519
As the rarest commercially-released NES game,
Stadium Events only hit a few stores in 1987

00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:05.479
before Nintendo pulled the game and re-released
it with its new accessory, the Power Pad.

00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:10.020
The new game became World Class Track Meet,
which was coded to work with the new motion

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.020
controller pad.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.490
It’s rumored that only about 200 copies
of the original Stadium Events sold before

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:20.890
the rest were destroyed, so those that have
been found are worth up to $10,000.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:24.310
Before you get too excited, only the US version
is rare.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:29.220
The UK's PAL version, with the white, oval
Nintendo logo, is pretty common and isn't

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.090
worth as much; the US version, which features
a circular Nintendo logo, is the one you’re

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:35.360
looking for.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.570
Copies of this crazy rare game are still known
to pop up at thrift shops, so keep your eyes

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:41.679
open and your legs limber.

00:01:41.679 --> 00:01:44.579
The Flintstones: Surprise At Dinosaur Peak

00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:49.729
By the time Surprise at Dinosaur Peak was
released in 1994, gamers were already three

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:54.640
years into the life-changing Super Nintendo
Entertainment System and had mostly lost interest

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.049
in 8-bit gaming.

00:01:56.049 --> 00:02:01.159
Not only was Dinosaur Peak one of the last
NES games to be released, but after 27 years

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:06.499
of reruns, interest in The Flintstones wasn't
exactly hot during this time either, despite

00:02:06.499 --> 00:02:08.950
ridiculous attempts to revitalize the franchise.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:13.700
If you happened to be a devotee of either
dying home consoles or caveman hijinks, you

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.250
may have hit the jackpot.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:19.030
This game sells for around 650 bones today.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:20.630
Little Samson

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:24.490
Another game released around the end of the
Nintendo Entertainment System's lifespan,

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:29.860
Little Samson saw poor sales in 1992, and
wasn't noticed by players until many years

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:30.860
later.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.190
In addition to being rare, it's one of the
few valuable games that's actually a lot of

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:38.470
fun to play, with critics comparing it to
Adventure Island and Mega Man.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:43.320
If you have a copy, it’s worth around $900,
but most authentic retro gamers will never

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:44.640
know the pleasure.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.780
Outback Joey

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.280
Before the Wii Fit tried to get tubby gamers
off the couch, there was Sega's Heartbeat

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.820
Personal Trainer, a rare variation of the
Sega Genesis console which incorporated movement-based

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.490
controls instead of the usual gamepad.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:02.750
Only one thousand Heartbeat Personal Trainers
were produced, and each came bundled with

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:07.290
Outback Joey, a game that monitored your heartbeat
to control a kangaroo.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:11.700
Decades ahead of the obesity epidemic, the
Heartbeat Personal Trainer missed the usual

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.680
gaming audience and remains relatively obscure.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:20.820
Outback Joey still slipped into a few collections
and is now worth around $1,950.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:21.870
Air Raid

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:26.490
The vast majority of Atari games don't have
any real value today, but if you have a copy

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:30.680
of Air Raid for the Atari 2600, you have a
piece of history.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.180
No one knows how many copies were released,
but only 15 have been found, and the company

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:38.670
that produced it, Men-a-Vision, never made
another game.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:43.950
Boxed copies of this UFO-invader game have
sold for over $30,000, but even loose carts

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:45.600
catch around three thousand.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.060
The cartridge's unique color and shape could
help it stand out from the local flea market’s

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.150
unending bounty of tube socks.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.871
Most Atari games aren’t worth the plastic
they’re made with today, but there’s at

00:03:55.871 --> 00:03:58.180
least one more exception to the rule…

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.690
Eli's Ladder

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:04.210
If you really want to make bank, build a time
machine, and use it to buy weird and educational

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.210
video games.

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:10.890
Eli's Ladder for the Atari 2600 is another
rare example of a valuable Atari title which

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:15.840
originally saw limited sales, mostly because
it taught math instead of making things explode

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.070
with spaceship lasers.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.850
Today, a copy is worth around $1,500.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:24.560
Its developer, Simage, never made any other
games, but the one they did make left a huge

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.470
impression on collectors and math-challenged
children.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:28.530
Magical Chase

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:33.230
A side-scroller starring an adorable witch,
Magical Chase was released at the very end

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:36.850
of the TurboGrafx-16's production in 1993.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:40.830
While the game was good enough to be re-released
on other platforms so that everyone could

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:46.080
enjoy it, collectors are still willing to
pay up to 4,000 dollars for the original release,

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.650
which sold poorly during its initial run in
stores.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:53.320
We’re not blaming Bette Midler for ruining
witches for everyone, but…we’re blaming

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:54.320
Bette Midler.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:57.650
"I put a spell on you!"

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:01.630
The Legend Of Zelda: Majora's Mask, Gray “Not
For Resale” Version

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:05.470
If you were friends with someone who worked
at a Toys "R" Us back in the early 2000s,

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:09.940
they may have been sent home with a copy of
Majora's Mask from their in-store Nintendo

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:11.900
64 demo machines.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:16.240
Unfortunately for gamers, most of these demo
games were incomplete prototypes and only

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:19.550
designed for a few minutes of in-store play
at a time.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:24.820
Fortunately for your wallet, many modern collectors
are completists, and Zelda fanatics are typically

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:26.430
even more intense.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:30.520
Copies of Majora’s Mask that are both grey
and bear the ‘not for resale’ label can

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:33.030
be worth up to $1,300.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.200
Working retail never looked so rewarding.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.360
Thanks for watching!

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.900
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:44.370
And leave us a comment to let us know which
rare games you’ve found in your closet...

